## sn3.19
sn3.19:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: Near Sāvatthī.
sn3.19:1.2 | S: Then King Pasenadi of Kosala went up to the Buddha in the middle of the day, bowed, and sat down to one side. The Buddha said to him, | T: Then King Pasenadi Kosala went to the Blessed One in the middle of the day and, on arrival, having bowed down to the Blessed One, sat to one side. As he was sitting there the Blessed One said to him,
sn3.19:1.3 | S: “So, Great King, where are you coming from in the middle of the day?” | T: “Well now, great king, where are you coming from in the middle of the day?”
sn3.19:2.1 | S: “Sir, here in Sāvatthī a financier householder has passed away. | T: “Just now, lord, a money-lending householder died in Sāvatthī.
sn3.19:2.2 | S: Since he died childless, I have come after transferring his fortune to the royal compound. | T: I have come from conveying his heirless fortune to the royal palace:
sn3.19:2.3 | S: There was eight million in gold coin, not to mention the silver coin. | T: eight million in silver, to say nothing of the gold.
sn3.19:2.4 | S: And yet that financier ate meals of | T: But even though he was a money-lending householder, his enjoyment of food was like this: He ate broken rice & pickle brine.
sn3.19:2.5 | S: sour gruel with false black pepper. | T: (none)
sn3.19:2.6 | S: He wore clothes consisting of | T: His enjoyment of clothing was like this: He wore three lengths of hempen cloth.
sn3.19:2.7 | S: three patches of sunn hemp. | T: (none)
sn3.19:2.8 | S: He traveled around in a vehicle that was | T: His enjoyment of a vehicle was like this:
sn3.19:2.9 | S: a dilapidated little cart, holding a leaf as parasol.” | T: He rode in a dilapidated little cart with an awning of leaves.”
sn3.19:3.1 | S: “That’s so true, Great King! That’s so true! | T: “That’s the way it is, great king. That’s the way it is.
sn3.19:3.2 | S: When an untrue person has acquired notable wealth they don’t make themselves happy and pleased. Nor do they make their mother and father, partners and children, bondservants, workers, and staff, and friends and colleagues happy and pleased. And they don’t establish an uplifting religious donation for ascetics and brahmins that’s conducive to heaven, ripens in happiness, and leads to heaven. | T: When a person of no integrity acquires lavish wealth, he doesn’t provide for his own pleasure & satisfaction, nor for the pleasure & satisfaction of his parents, nor for the pleasure & satisfaction of his wife & children; nor for the pleasure & satisfaction of his slaves, servants, & assistants; nor for the pleasure & satisfaction of his friends. He doesn’t institute for contemplatives & brahmans offerings of supreme aim, heavenly, resulting in happiness, leading to heaven.
sn3.19:3.3 | S: Because they haven’t made proper use of that wealth, rulers or bandits take it, or fire consumes it, or flood sweeps it away, or unloved heirs take it. | T: When his wealth isn’t properly put to use, kings make off with it, or thieves make off with it, or fire burns it, or water sweeps it away, or hateful heirs make off with it.
sn3.19:3.4 | S: Since that wealth is not properly utilized, it’s wasted, not used. | T: Thus his wealth, not properly put to use, goes to waste and not to any good use.
sn3.19:4.1 | S: Suppose there was a lotus pond in an uninhabited region with clear, sweet, cool water, clean, with smooth banks, delightful. | T: “Just as with a pond in a place haunted by non-human beings, with clear water, cool water, fresh water, clean, with good fords, delightful:
sn3.19:4.2 | S: But people don’t collect it or drink it or bathe in it or use it for any purpose. | T: No people would draw water from it or drink it or bathe in it or apply it to their needs.
sn3.19:4.3 | S: Since that water is not properly utilized, it’s wasted, not used. | T: And so that water, not properly put to use, would go to waste and not to any good use.
sn3.19:4.4 | S: In the same way, when an untrue person has acquired notable wealth … | T: In the same way, when a person of no integrity acquires lavish wealth…
sn3.19:4.5 | P: Tassa te bhoge evaṁ sammā aparibhuñjiyamāne rājāno vā haranti, corā vā haranti, aggi vā ḍahati, udakaṁ vā vahati, appiyā vā dāyādā haranti. | T: his wealth, not properly put to use,
sn3.19:4.6 | S: it’s wasted, not used. | T: goes to waste and not to any good use.
sn3.19:5.1 | S: When a true person has acquired notable wealth they make themselves happy and pleased. And they make their mother and father, partners and children, bondservants, workers, and staff, and friends and colleagues happy and pleased. And they establish an uplifting religious donation for ascetics and brahmins that’s conducive to heaven, ripens in happiness, and leads to heaven. | T: “But when a person of integrity acquires lavish wealth, he provides for his own pleasure & satisfaction, for the pleasure & satisfaction of his parents, the pleasure & satisfaction of his wife & children; the pleasure & satisfaction of his slaves, servants, & assistants; and the pleasure & satisfaction of his friends. He institutes for contemplatives & brahmans offerings of supreme aim, heavenly, resulting in happiness, leading to heaven.
sn3.19:5.2 | S: Because they make proper use of that wealth, rulers or bandits don’t take it, fire doesn’t consume it, flood doesn’t sweep it away, and unloved heirs don’t take it. | T: When his wealth is properly put to use, kings don’t make off with it, thieves don’t make off with it, fire doesn’t burn it, water doesn’t sweep it away, and hateful heirs don’t make off with it.
sn3.19:5.3 | S: Since that wealth is properly utilized, it’s used, not wasted. | T: Thus his wealth, properly put to use, goes to a good use and not to waste.
sn3.19:6.1 | S: Suppose there was a lotus pond not far from a town or village with clear, sweet, cool water, clean, with smooth banks, delightful. | T: “Just as with a pond not far from a town or village, with clear water, cool water, fresh water, clean, with good fords, delightful.
sn3.19:6.2 | S: And people collected it and drank it and bathed in it and used it for their own purpose. | T: People would draw water from it or drink it or bathe in it or apply it to their needs.
sn3.19:6.3 | S: Since that water is properly utilized, it’s used, not wasted. | T: And so that water, properly put to use, would go to a good use and not to waste.
sn3.19:6.4 | S: In the same way, when a true person has acquired notable wealth … | T: In the same way, when a person of integrity acquires lavish wealth…
sn3.19:6.5 | P: Tassa te bhoge evaṁ sammā paribhuñjiyamāne neva rājāno haranti, na corā haranti, na aggi ḍahati, na udakaṁ vahati, na appiyā dāyādā haranti. | T: his wealth, properly put to use,
sn3.19:6.6 | S: it’s used, not wasted. | T: goes to a good use and not to waste.”
sn3.19:7.1 | S: As cool water in an uninhabited region | T: That is what the Blessed One said. Having said that, the One Well-Gone, the Teacher, said further: “Like water in a haunted place
sn3.19:7.2 | S: evaporates when not drunk; | T: that, without being imbibed, dries up:
sn3.19:7.3 | S: so too when a reprobate acquires wealth, | T: Such is the wealth acquired by a worthless man
sn3.19:7.4 | S: they neither use it themselves nor give it away. | T: who neither enjoys it himself nor gives.
sn3.19:8.1 | S: When the attentive and sensible acquire wealth, | T: But one enlightened & knowing, on acquiring wealth,
sn3.19:8.2 | S: they use it, being dutiful. | T: enjoys it & performs his duties.
sn3.19:8.3 | S: That boss, having supported the family unit, | T: He, a bull among men, having supported his kin
sn3.19:8.4 | S: blameless, goes to a heavenly place.” | T: without blame, without blame goes to the land of heaven.”

## an7.61
an7.61:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: (none)
an7.61:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Bhaggas at Crocodile’s Bellow, in the deer park at Bhesakaḷā’s Wood. | T: Once the Blessed One was living among the Bhaggas in the Deer Park at Bhesakaḷā Forest, near Crocodile Haunt.
an7.61:1.3 | S: Now at that time, in the land of the Magadhans near Kallavāḷamutta Village, Venerable Mahāmoggallāna was nodding off while meditating. | T: At that time Ven. Mahā Moggallāna [prior to his awakening] sat nodding near the village of Kallavālamutta, in Magadha.
an7.61:1.4 | S: The Buddha saw him with his clairvoyance that is purified and superhuman. | T: The Blessed One, with his purified divine eye, surpassing the human, saw Ven. Mahā Moggallāna as he sat nodding near the village of Kallavālamutta in Magadha.
an7.61:1.5 | P: Disvā— | T: As soon as he saw this—
an7.61:1.6 | S: Then, as easily as a strong person would extend or contract their arm, he vanished from the deer park at Bhesakaḷā’s Wood in the land of the Bhaggas and reappeared in front of Mahāmoggallāna near Kallavāḷamutta Village in the land of the Magadhans. | T: just as a strong man might extend his flexed arm or flex his extended arm—he disappeared from among the Bhaggas in the Deer Park at Bhesakaḷā Forest near Crocodile Haunt and re-appeared near the village of Kallavālamutta in Magadha, right in front of Ven. Mahā Moggallāna.
an7.61:1.7 | S: He sat down on the seat spread out | T: There he sat down on a prepared seat.
an7.61:1.8 | S: and said to Mahāmoggallāna, | T: As he was sitting there, the Blessed One said to Ven. Mahā Moggallāna,
an7.61:2.1 | S: “Are you nodding off, Moggallāna? Are you nodding off?” | T: “Are you nodding, Moggallāna? Are you nodding?”
an7.61:2.2 | S: “Yes, sir.” | T: “Yes, lord.”
an7.61:2.3 | S: “So, Moggallāna, don’t focus on or cultivate the perception that you were meditating on when you fell drowsy. | T: “Well then, Moggallāna, whatever perception you have in mind when drowsiness descends on you, don’t attend to that perception, don’t pursue it.
an7.61:2.4 | S: It’s possible that you’ll give up drowsiness in this way. | T: It’s possible that by doing this you will shake off your drowsiness.
an7.61:3.1 | S: But what if that doesn’t work? Then think about and consider the teaching as you’ve learned and memorized it, examining it with your mind. | T: “But if by doing this you don’t shake off your drowsiness, then recall to your awareness the Dhamma as you have heard & memorized it, re-examine it, & ponder it over in your mind.
an7.61:3.2 | S: It’s possible that you’ll give up drowsiness in this way. | T: It’s possible that by doing this you will shake off your drowsiness.
an7.61:4.1 | S: But what if that doesn’t work? Then rehearse in detail the teaching as you’ve learned and memorized it. | T: “But if by doing this you don’t shake off your drowsiness, then repeat aloud in detail the Dhamma as you have heard & memorized it.
an7.61:4.2 | S: It’s possible that you’ll give up drowsiness in this way. | T: It’s possible that by doing this you will shake off your drowsiness.
an7.61:5.1 | S: But what if that doesn’t work? Then pinch your ears and rub your limbs. | T: “But if by doing this you don’t shake off your drowsiness, then pull both your earlobes and rub your limbs with your hands.
an7.61:5.2 | S: It’s possible that you’ll give up drowsiness in this way. | T: It’s possible that by doing this you will shake off your drowsiness.
an7.61:6.1 | S: But what if that doesn’t work? Then get up from your seat, flush your eyes with water, look around in every direction, and look up at the stars and constellations. | T: “But if by doing this you don’t shake off your drowsiness, then get up from your seat and, after washing your eyes out with water, look around in all directions and upward to the major stars & constellations.
an7.61:6.2 | S: It’s possible that you’ll give up drowsiness in this way. | T: It’s possible that by doing this you will shake off your drowsiness.
an7.61:7.1 | S: But what if that doesn’t work? Then apply your mind to the perception of light, focusing on the perception of day: | T: “But if by doing this you don’t shake off your drowsiness, then attend to the perception of light, resolve on the perception of daytime,
an7.61:7.2 | S: as by day, so by night; as by night, so by day. | T: (dwelling) by night as by day, and by day as by night.
an7.61:7.3 | S: And so, with an open and unenveloped heart, develop a mind that’s full of radiance. | T: By means of an awareness thus open & unhampered, develop a brightened mind.
an7.61:7.4 | S: It’s possible that you’ll give up drowsiness in this way. | T: It’s possible that by doing this you will shake off your drowsiness.
an7.61:8.1 | S: But what if that doesn’t work? Then walk mindfully, focusing on the perception of before and behind, your faculties directed inwards and your mind not scattered outside. | T: “But if by doing this you don’t shake off your drowsiness, then—percipient of what lies in front & behind—set a distance to meditate walking back & forth, your senses inwardly immersed, your mind not straying outwards.
an7.61:8.2 | S: It’s possible that you’ll give up drowsiness in this way. | T: It’s possible that by doing this you will shake off your drowsiness.
an7.61:9.1 | S: But what if that doesn’t work? Then lie down in the lion’s posture—on the right side, placing one foot on top of the other—mindful and aware, and focused on the time of getting up. | T: “But if by doing this you don’t shake off your drowsiness, then—reclining on your right side—take up the lion’s posture, one foot placed on top of the other, mindful, alert, with your mind set on getting up.
an7.61:9.2 | S: When you wake, you should get up quickly, thinking: | T: As soon as you wake up, get up quickly, with the thought,
an7.61:9.3 | S: ‘I will not live indulging in the pleasures of sleeping, lying down, and drowsing.’ | T: ‘I won’t stay indulging in the pleasure of lying down, the pleasure of reclining, the pleasure of drowsiness.’
an7.61:9.4 | S: That’s how you should train. | T: That is how you should train yourself.
an7.61:10.1 | S: So you should train like this: | T: “And further, Moggallāna, should you train yourself:
an7.61:10.2 | S: ‘I will not approach families with my head swollen with vanity.’ | T: ‘I will not visit families with my pride [literally: my trunk (i.e., an elephant’s trunk)] lifted high.’
an7.61:10.3 | S: That’s how you should train. | T: That is how you should train yourself.
an7.61:10.4 | S: What happens if a mendicant approaches families with a head swollen with vanity? Well, families have business to attend to, | T: Among families there are many jobs that have to be done, so that people don’t pay attention to a visiting monk. If a monk visits them with his trunk lifted high, the thought will occur to him,
an7.61:10.5 | S: so people might not notice when a mendicant arrives. In that case the mendicant thinks: | T: (none)
an7.61:10.6 | S: ‘Who on earth has turned this family against me? It seems they don’t like me any more.’ | T: ‘Now who, I wonder, has caused a split between me and this family? The people seem to have no liking for me.’
an7.61:10.7 | S: And so, because they don’t get anything they feel dismayed. Being dismayed, they become restless. Being restless, they lose restraint. And without restraint the mind is far from immersion. | T: Getting nothing, he becomes abashed. Abashed, he becomes restless. Restless, he becomes unrestrained. Unrestrained, his mind is far from concentration.
an7.61:11.1 | S: So you should train like this: | T: “And further, Moggallāna, should you train yourself:
an7.61:11.2 | S: ‘I won’t get into arguments.’ | T: ‘I will speak no confrontational speech.’
an7.61:11.3 | S: That’s how you should train. | T: That is how you should train yourself.
an7.61:11.4 | S: When there’s an argument, you can expect there’ll be lots of talking. When there’s lots of talking, people become restless. Being restless, they lose restraint. And without restraint the mind is far from immersion. | T: When there is confrontational speech, a lot of discussion can be expected. When there is a lot of discussion, there is restlessness. One who is restless becomes unrestrained. Unrestrained, his mind is far from concentration.
an7.61:11.5 | S: Moggallāna, I don’t praise all kinds of closeness. | T: “It’s not the case, Moggallāna, that I praise association of every sort.
an7.61:11.6 | S: Nor do I criticize all kinds of closeness. | T: But it’s not the case that I dispraise association of every sort.
an7.61:11.7 | S: I don’t praise closeness with laypeople and renunciates. | T: I don’t praise association with householders and renunciates.
an7.61:11.8 | S: I do praise closeness with those lodgings that are quiet and still, far from the madding crowd, remote from human settlements, and fit for retreat.” | T: But as for dwelling places that are free from noise, free from sound, their atmosphere devoid of people, appropriately secluded for resting undisturbed by human beings: I praise association with dwelling places of this sort.”
an7.61:12.1 | S: When he said this, Venerable Moggallāna asked the Buddha, | T: When this was said, Ven. Moggallāna said to the Blessed One:
an7.61:12.2 | S: “Sir, how do you briefly define a mendicant who is freed through the ending of craving, who has reached the ultimate end, the ultimate sanctuary from the yoke, the ultimate spiritual life, the ultimate goal, and is best among gods and humans?” | T: “Briefly, lord, in what respect is a monk released through the ending of craving, utterly complete, utterly free from bonds, a follower of the utterly holy life, utterly consummate: foremost among devas & human beings?”
an7.61:13.1 | S: “Take a mendicant who has heard: | T: “There is the case, Moggallāna, where a monk has heard,
an7.61:13.2 | S: ‘Nothing is worth insisting on.’ | T: ‘All dhammas are unworthy of adherence.’
an7.61:13.3 | S: When a mendicant has heard that | T: Having heard that all dhammas are unworthy of adherence,
an7.61:13.4 | S: nothing is worth insisting on, | T: (none)
an7.61:13.5 | S: they directly know all things. Directly knowing all things, they completely understand all things. Completely understanding all things, when they experience any kind of feeling—pleasant, unpleasant, or neutral— | T: he directly knows every dhamma. Directly knowing every dhamma, he comprehends every dhamma. Comprehending every dhamma, then whatever feeling he experiences—pleasure, pain, neither pleasure nor pain—
an7.61:13.6 | S: they meditate observing impermanence, dispassion, cessation, and letting go in those feelings. | T: he remains focused on inconstancy, focused on dispassion, focused on cessation, focused on relinquishing with regard to that feeling.
an7.61:13.7 | S: Meditating in this way, they don’t grasp at anything in the world. | T: As he remains focused on inconstancy, focused on dispassion, focused on cessation, focused on relinquishing with regard to that feeling, he is unsustained by [doesn’t cling to] anything in the world.
an7.61:13.8 | S: Not grasping, they’re not anxious. Not being anxious, they personally become extinguished. | T: Unsustained, he isn’t agitated. Unagitated, he totally unbinds right within.
an7.61:13.9 | S: They understand: ‘Rebirth is ended, the spiritual journey has been completed, what had to be done has been done, there is nothing further for this place.’ | T: He discerns: ‘Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for this world.’
an7.61:13.10 | S: That’s how I briefly define a mendicant who is freed through the ending of craving, who has reached the ultimate end, the ultimate sanctuary from the yoke, the ultimate spiritual life, the ultimate goal, and is best among gods and humans.” | T: “It’s in this respect, Moggallāna, that a monk, in brief, is released through the ending of craving, utterly complete, utterly free from bonds, a follower of the utterly holy life, utterly consummate: foremost among devas & human beings.”
an7.61:13.11 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## snp1.7
snp1.7:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
snp1.7:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
snp1.7:1.3 | S: Then the Buddha robed up in the morning and, taking his bowl and robe, entered Sāvatthī for alms. | T: Then, in the early morning, after adjusting his lower robe and carrying his bowl & outer robe, he entered Sāvatthī for alms.
snp1.7:1.4 | S: Now at that time in the brahmin Bhāradvāja the Fire-Worshiper’s home the sacred flame had been kindled and the oblation prepared. | T: Now at that time, in the house of the brahman Aggika Bhāradvāja, a (sacrificial) fire was burning and an offering was lifted up.
snp1.7:1.5 | S: Wandering indiscriminately for almsfood in Sāvatthī, the Buddha approached Bhāradvāja the Fire-Worshiper’s house. | T: Then the Blessed One, going through Sāvatthī on a methodical almsround, approached the house of Aggika Bhāradvāja.
snp1.7:2.1 | S: Bhāradvāja the Fire-Worshiper saw the Buddha coming off in the distance | T: Aggika Bhāradvāja saw the Blessed One coming from afar and,
snp1.7:2.2 | S: and said to him, | T: on seeing him, said to him:
snp1.7:2.3 | S: “Stop right there, shaveling! | T: “Stop right there, you little shaveling!
snp1.7:2.4 | S: Right there, fake ascetic! | T: Right there, you little contemplative!
snp1.7:2.5 | S: Right there, lowlife!” | T: Right there, you little outcaste!”
snp1.7:3.1 | S: When he said this, the Buddha said to him, | T: When this was said, the Blessed One said to the brahman Aggika Bhāradvāja,
snp1.7:3.2 | S: “But brahmin, do you know what is a lowlife or what are the qualities that make you a lowlife?” | T: “But do you know, brahman, what an outcaste is, or the actions that make one an outcaste?”
snp1.7:3.3 | S: “No I do not, worthy Gotama. | T: “No, in fact, Master Gotama, I don’t know what an outcaste is, or the actions that make one an outcaste.
snp1.7:3.4 | S: Please, worthy Gotama, teach me this matter so I can understand what is a lowlife or what are the qualities that make you a lowlife.” | T: It would be good if Master Gotama taught me the Dhamma so that I would know what an outcaste is and the actions that make one an outcaste.”
snp1.7:3.5 | S: “Well then, brahmin, listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: “In that case, brahman, listen and pay close attention. I will speak.”
snp1.7:3.6 | S: “Yes, worthy sir,” Bhāradvāja the Fire-Worshiper replied. | T: “As you say, master,” the brahman Aggika Bhāradvāja responded to the Blessed One.
snp1.7:3.7 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said:
snp1.7:4.1 | S: “Irritable and acrimonious, | T: Whatever man is angry, resentful,
snp1.7:4.2 | S: wicked and offensive, | T: evil, merciless,
snp1.7:4.3 | S: a man deficient in view, deceitful: | T: deceitful, and defective in his views:
snp1.7:4.4 | S: know him as a lowlife. | T: He should be known as ‘outcaste.’

