snp1.7:22.2 | S: or his disciple, | T: or his disciple,
snp1.7:22.3 | S: whether lay or renunciate: | T: wanderer or householder:
snp1.7:22.4 | S: know him as a lowlife. | T: He should be known as ‘outcaste.’
snp1.7:23.1 | S: He claims to be a perfected one, | T: Whoever, though not an arahant, claims to be an arahant:
snp1.7:23.2 | S: when he really is no such thing. | T: (none)
snp1.7:23.3 | S: In the world with its divinities, | T: He is the thief in this world with its Brahmās.
snp1.7:23.4 | S: that crook is truly the lowest lowlife. | T: He is the vilest of outcastes.
snp1.7:23.5 | S: These who are called lowlifes | T: These are said to be outcastes,
snp1.7:23.6 | S: I have revealed to you. | T: as I have proclaimed them to you.
snp1.7:24.1 | S: You’re not a lowlife by birth, | T: Not by birth is one an outcaste,
snp1.7:24.2 | S: nor by birth are you a brahmin. | T: not by birth a brahman.
snp1.7:24.3 | S: You’re a lowlife by your deeds, | T: By action one is an outcaste.
snp1.7:24.4 | S: by deeds you’re a brahmin. | T: By action is one a brahman.
snp1.7:25.1 | S: And also you should know | T: Know, too, by this,
snp1.7:25.2 | S: according to this example. | T: as I give an example:
snp1.7:25.3 | S: A corpse-worker’s son of the dog-eating caste | T: Sopāka, the son of an outcaste,
snp1.7:25.4 | S: became renowned as Mātaṅga. | T: was well-known as Mātaṅga:
