snp1.7:28.2 | S: who recite as kinsmen of the hymns, | T: brahmans, with chants as their kinsmen,
snp1.7:28.3 | S: are often discovered | T: are repeatedly seen with evil deeds:
snp1.7:28.4 | S: in the midst of wicked deeds. | T: (none)
snp1.7:29.1 | S: Blameworthy in this very life, | T: blameworthy in the here-&-now,
snp1.7:29.2 | S: and in lives to come, a bad destination. | T: with a bad destination in the afterlife.
snp1.7:29.3 | S: Their birth does not prevent them | T: Their birth doesn’t prevent them
snp1.7:29.4 | S: from blame or bad destiny. | T: from blame & a bad destination.
snp1.7:30.1 | S: You’re not a lowlife by birth, | T: Not by birth is one an outcaste,
snp1.7:30.2 | S: nor by birth are you a brahmin. | T: not by birth a brahman.
snp1.7:30.3 | S: You’re a lowlife by your deeds, | T: By action one is an outcaste.
snp1.7:30.4 | S: by deeds you’re a brahmin.” | T: By action is one a brahman.
snp1.7:31.1 | S: When he had spoken, the brahmin Bhāradvāja the Fire-Worshiper said to the Buddha, | T: When this was said, the brahman Aggika Bhāradvāja said to the Blessed One:
snp1.7:31.2 | S: “Excellent, worthy Gotama! Excellent! … | T: “Magnificent, Master Gotama! Magnificent! Just as if he were to place upright what was overturned, to reveal what was hidden, to show the way to one who was lost, or to carry a lamp into the dark so that those with eyes could see forms, in the same way has Master Gotama—through many lines of reasoning—made the Dhamma clear. I go to Master Gotama for refuge, to the Dhamma, & to the Saṅgha of monks.
snp1.7:31.3 | S: From this day forth, may the worthy Gotama remember me as a lay follower who has gone for refuge for life.” | T: May Master Gotama remember me as a lay follower who has gone for refuge from this day forward, for life.”
snp1.7:31.4 | P: Vasalasuttaṁ sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## snp5.9
snp5.9:1.1 | S: “Those who have previously answered me,” | T: In the past,
snp5.9:1.2 | S: said Venerable Hemaka, | T: (none)
snp5.9:1.3 | S: “before Gotama’s teaching, | T: before hearing Gotama’s message,
snp5.9:1.4 | S: said ‘thus it was’ or ‘so it shall be’. | T: when anyone explained ‘It is,’ ‘It will be,’
snp5.9:1.5 | S: All that was just the testament of hearsay; | T: all that was hearsay, quotation marks.
snp5.9:1.6 | S: all that just fostered speculation: | T: All that promoted conjecture
snp5.9:1.7 | S: I found no delight in that. | T: and gave me no pleasure.
snp5.9:2.1 | S: But you, sage, explain to me | T: Now, sage, teach me
snp5.9:2.2 | S: the teaching that destroys craving. | T: the Dhamma demolishing craving,
snp5.9:2.3 | S: Having understood it, one who lives mindfully | T: knowing which, living mindfully,
snp5.9:2.4 | S: may cross over clinging in the world.” | T: one would cross over beyond entanglement in the world.
snp5.9:3.1 | S: “The removal of desire and lust, Hemaka, | T: The Buddha: Here, Hemaka,
snp5.9:3.2 | S: for pleasant things | T: with regard to things dear
snp5.9:3.3 | S: seen, heard, thought, or cognized here | T: —seen, heard, sensed, & cognized— there is: the dispelling of passion & desire,
snp5.9:3.4 | S: is extinguishment, the state that does not pass. | T: the unfallen, undying state of unbinding.
snp5.9:4.1 | S: Those who have fully understood this, mindful, | T: Those knowing this, mindful,
snp5.9:4.2 | S: are quenched in this very life. | T: fully unbound in the here-&-now,
snp5.9:4.3 | S: Always at peace, | T: are always calmed,
snp5.9:4.4 | S: they’ve crossed over clinging to the world.” | T: have crossed over beyond entanglement in the world.
snp5.9:4.5 | P: Hemakamāṇavapucchā aṭṭhamā. | T: (none)

## sn22.2
sn22.2:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
sn22.2:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Sakyans, where they have a town named Devadaha. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying among the Sakyans at a Sakyan town named Devadaha.
sn22.2:1.3 | S: Then several mendicants who were heading for the west went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him, | T: Then a large number of monks headed for outlying districts went to the Blessed One and on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As they were sitting there they said to the Blessed One,
sn22.2:1.4 | S: “Sir, we wish to go to a western land to take up residence there.” | T: “Lord, we want to go to the countryside of the outlying districts and to take up residence there.”
sn22.2:2.1 | S: “But mendicants, have you taken leave of Sāriputta?” | T: “Have you informed Sāriputta?”
sn22.2:2.2 | S: “No, sir, we haven’t.” | T: “No, lord, we haven’t informed Ven. Sāriputta.”
sn22.2:2.3 | S: “You should take leave of Sāriputta. | T: “Inform Sāriputta, monks.
sn22.2:2.4 | S: He’s astute, and supports his spiritual companions, the mendicants.” | T: Sāriputta is wise, a great help to the monks who are his companions in the holy life.”
sn22.2:2.5 | S: “Yes, sir,” they replied. | T: “As you say, lord,” the monks responded to the Blessed One.
sn22.2:3.1 | S: Now at that time Venerable Sāriputta was sitting not far from the Buddha in a clump of golden shower trees. | T: At that time Ven. Sāriputta was sitting under a certain cassia tree not far from the Blessed One.
sn22.2:3.2 | S: And then those mendicants approved and agreed with what the Buddha said. They got up from their seat, bowed and respectfully circled the Buddha, keeping him on their right. Then they went up to Venerable Sāriputta, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then the monks, delighting in & approving of the Blessed One’s words, rose from their seats and—bowing down to the Blessed One and circumambulating him, keeping him to their right—went to Ven. Sāriputta. On arrival, they exchanged courteous greetings with him.
sn22.2:3.3 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, they sat down to one side and said to him, | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, they sat to one side. As they were sitting there, they said to Ven. Sāriputta,
sn22.2:3.4 | S: “Reverend Sāriputta, we wish to go to a western land to take up residence there. | T: “Friend Sāriputta, we want to go to the countryside of the outlying districts and to take up residence there.
sn22.2:3.5 | S: We have taken leave of the Teacher.” | T: We have already informed the Teacher.”
sn22.2:4.1 | S: “Reverends, there are those who question a mendicant who has gone abroad— | T: “Friends, in foreign lands there are wise nobles & brahmans, householders & contemplatives—for the people there are wise & discriminating—who will question a monk:
sn22.2:4.2 | S: astute aristocrats, brahmins, householders, and ascetics— | T: (none)
sn22.2:4.3 | S: for astute people are inquisitive: | T: (none)
sn22.2:4.4 | S: ‘But what does the venerables’ Teacher teach? What does he explain?’ I trust the venerables have properly heard, learned, applied the mind, and remembered the teachings, and penetrated them with wisdom. That way, when answering you will repeat what the Buddha has said and not misrepresent him with an untruth. You will explain in line with the teaching, with no legitimate grounds for rebuttal and criticism.” | T: ‘What is your teacher’s doctrine? What does he teach?’ Have you listened well to the teachings—grasped them well, attended to them well, considered them well, penetrated them well by means of discernment—so that in answering you will speak in line with what the Blessed One has said, will not misrepresent the Blessed One with what is unfactual, will answer in line with the Dhamma, and no one whose thinking is in line with the Dhamma will have grounds for criticizing you?”
sn22.2:5.1 | S: “Reverend, we would travel a long way to learn the meaning of this statement in the presence of Venerable Sāriputta. | T: “We would come from a long way away to hear the explication of these words in Ven. Sāriputta’s presence.
sn22.2:5.2 | S: May Venerable Sāriputta himself please clarify the meaning of this.” | T: It would be good if Ven. Sāriputta himself would enlighten us as to their meaning.”
sn22.2:5.3 | S: “Well then, reverends, listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: “Then in that case, friends, listen & pay close attention. I will speak.”
sn22.2:5.4 | S: “Yes, reverend,” they replied. | T: “As you say, friend,” the monks responded to him.
sn22.2:5.5 | S: Sāriputta said this: | T: Ven. Sāriputta said:
